1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,093
Welcome to the complete S I T setup and user guide.

2
00:00:05,093 --> 00:00:08,334
S I T means Smart Interactive Transcriber.

3
00:00:08,334 --> 00:00:14,816
It keeps recordings, speaker-labelled transcripts, and structured
meeting analysis together in one local library.

4
00:00:14,816 --> 00:00:21,761
This guide uses the current zero point one point zero alpha release on
Arch Linux.

5
00:00:21,761 --> 00:00:35,651
We will install the application, configure its managed SSH connections,
record and upload audio, generate a transcript with remote Spark
services, review speakers, create AI analysis, and manage the library.

6
00:00:35,651 --> 00:00:39,355
The model services themselves are outside this guide.

7
00:00:39,355 --> 00:00:46,300
Working remote endpoints are assumed, and this demonstration will not
use the local transcription fallback.

8
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:56,876
S I T has a FastAPI backend and the same web interface in a normal
browser or a native Tauri window.

9
00:00:56,876 --> 00:01:05,513
On supported Linux audio systems, the recorder captures the microphone
and the selected output monitor as separate channels.

10
00:01:05,513 --> 00:01:11,751
Each channel is transcribed independently, which protects clean system
audio from room echo.

11
00:01:11,751 --> 00:01:16,070
Remote speech recognition and speaker recognition use Spark-compatible
services.

12
00:01:16,070 --> 00:01:19,429
AI analysis uses a separate OpenAI-compatible endpoint.

13
00:01:19,429 --> 00:01:26,626
The application is verified end to end on Arch Linux with PipeWire Pulse
or PulseAudio.

14
00:01:26,626 --> 00:01:34,303
Fedora, Ubuntu, Debian, and WSL two are documented, but not yet verified
to the same level.

15
00:01:34,303 --> 00:01:40,061
WSL users should expect recording limitations and can always use file
upload.

16
00:01:40,061 --> 00:01:43,900
Native Windows and macOS are not currently supported.

17
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,389
Start with system packages.

18
00:01:46,389 --> 00:01:51,858
On Arch Linux, install Git, curl, Python, uv, FFmpeg, and libpulse.

19
00:01:51,858 --> 00:01:58,322
Libpulse supplies the pactl client used to discover microphones,
outputs, and monitor sources.

20
00:01:58,322 --> 00:02:08,267
Keep the PipeWire Pulse or PulseAudio server already provided by your
desktop; do not replace it for S I T.

21
00:02:08,267 --> 00:02:14,233
If you only want the browser interface, the first command is enough.

22
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:24,675
For the native window, also install the compiler toolchain, WebKit GTK,
OpenSSL, the application indicator and SVG libraries, xdotool, and
rustup.

23
00:02:24,675 --> 00:02:27,658
Then select the stable Rust toolchain.

24
00:02:27,658 --> 00:02:33,128
Fedora and Ubuntu use equivalent packages listed in the project readme.

25
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:38,597
Use Python three point twelve when possible, or three point eleven.

26
00:02:38,597 --> 00:02:44,067
Newer rolling-release Python versions may not have every machine-
learning wheel available.

27
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:53,293
Clone the repository, enter the checkout, and create a dedicated Python
three point twelve environment named dot venv.

28
00:02:53,293 --> 00:02:56,687
Install requirements dot txt into that environment.

29
00:02:56,687 --> 00:03:02,020
This is the recommended remote-processing profile and keeps the
environment small.

30
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:14,626
It includes the application and the CPU ONNX transcription package, but
this tutorial will use the configured remote services and will not
demonstrate local fallback processing.

31
00:03:14,626 --> 00:03:18,504
The optional local Pyannote installation is intentionally omitted.

32
00:03:18,504 --> 00:03:22,383
Installing from the private repository requires GitHub access.

33
00:03:22,383 --> 00:03:31,594
The first model download, if local processing is ever enabled later,
also requires network access and additional disk space.

34
00:03:31,594 --> 00:03:38,867
Keep the checkout after installation because the desktop launcher points
to absolute paths inside it.

35
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:45,710
For the browser version, run the transcriber module and open the local
address shown here.

36
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:49,516
The backend remains active until you press Control C.

37
00:03:49,516 --> 00:03:55,437
For the native window, build the Tauri release binary and run the
desktop installer.

38
00:03:55,437 --> 00:03:57,974
The installer needs no sudo access.

39
00:03:57,974 --> 00:04:04,318
It creates a launcher in your local bin directory, a desktop entry, and
an icon.

40
00:04:04,318 --> 00:04:08,124
S I T then appears in the application menu.

41
00:04:08,124 --> 00:04:14,890
The native launcher can reuse an existing backend or start one directly;
systemd is not required.

42
00:04:14,890 --> 00:04:24,194
Closing a native window also stops a backend that the window started, so
wait for recordings and processing jobs to finish first.

43
00:04:24,194 --> 00:04:27,154
A manually started backend keeps running independently.

44
00:04:27,154 --> 00:04:34,767
The default library is stored below your X D G data directory, normally
dot local share slash sit.

45
00:04:35,267 --> 00:04:38,017
Open Connections from the left sidebar.

46
00:04:38,017 --> 00:04:41,683
Enable Reach the AI services through SSH tunnels.

47
00:04:41,683 --> 00:04:44,892
Enter the shared SSH host and user.

48
00:04:44,892 --> 00:04:47,183
Port twenty-two is normally correct.

49
00:04:47,183 --> 00:04:55,433
Leave SSH key empty to use your SSH agent, your normal SSH config, or
the default identity files.

50
00:04:55,433 --> 00:04:59,100
Otherwise enter the path to a private key.

51
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:02,767
S I T never stores an SSH password.

52
00:05:02,767 --> 00:05:07,350
Test SSH connection checks only login and port forwarding permission.

53
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:15,600
A green signed-in result proves that SSH works, but it does not yet
start all persistent service tunnels.

54
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:25,225
The application stores connection settings per user in dot local share
slash sit slash dot connections dot json with owner-only permissions.

55
00:05:25,225 --> 00:05:32,100
For the video, the real hosts and username are redacted; the connection
result is genuine.

56
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:36,859
Each service has a local port and a remote port.

57
00:05:36,859 --> 00:05:40,266
The local port is opened on this computer.

58
00:05:40,266 --> 00:05:45,377
The remote port is where the service listens on the remote machine.

59
00:05:45,377 --> 00:05:47,933
Every local port must be unique.

60
00:05:47,933 --> 00:05:53,470
In this setup, transcription uses local and remote port eight two zero
eight.

61
00:05:53,470 --> 00:05:56,877
Pyannote speaker recognition uses eight two nine nine.

62
00:05:56,877 --> 00:06:01,988
The analysis service uses local and remote port eight one zero zero.

63
00:06:01,988 --> 00:06:07,525
Its Advanced section overrides the SSH host because it runs on another
machine.

64
00:06:07,525 --> 00:06:12,210
The model field must match a model exposed by that server.

65
00:06:12,210 --> 00:06:22,432
The transcription API path is slash v one slash audio slash
transcriptions, and the diarization path is slash v one slash audio
slash diarizations.

66
00:06:22,432 --> 00:06:32,227
Advanced settings also support a different remote bind address, API
path, per-service SSH host, or a direct HTTP address when tunnels are
disabled.

67
00:06:32,227 --> 00:06:41,172
The Hugging Face token is only needed by the optional local Pyannote
fallback, so a remote-only deployment can leave it unused.

68
00:06:41,172 --> 00:06:49,690
Press Set up and test on one service to save the complete form, restart
the tunnels, and test that service.

69
00:06:49,690 --> 00:06:55,653
Press the main Set up and test button at the bottom to test everything.

70
00:06:55,653 --> 00:07:08,004
A healthy remote setup reports that the one-second transcription upload
was accepted, Pyannote is running on CUDA, and the configured G P T O S
S model is reachable.

71
00:07:08,004 --> 00:07:13,967
The diagnostics section also shows tunnel ownership, resolved endpoints,
model names, and actionable errors.

72
00:07:14,467 --> 00:07:18,515
Open Customization before the first real meeting.

73
00:07:18,515 --> 00:07:27,189
Choose a profile for meetings, interviews, lectures, or personal notes,
then adjust every default independently.

74
00:07:27,189 --> 00:07:35,863
Select the usual project, microphone, output monitor, recording
language, live-preview cadence, and expected speaker count.

75
00:07:35,863 --> 00:07:39,332
One expected speaker skips speaker separation.

76
00:07:39,332 --> 00:07:45,693
Decide whether live transcription and automatic speaker recognition
start by default.

77
00:07:45,693 --> 00:07:53,211
Reading preferences control the initial transcript view, paragraph
grouping, timestamps, and speaker labels.

78
00:07:53,211 --> 00:08:01,885
AI analysis defaults control detail, language, comparison with recent
meetings, and the sections to request.

79
00:08:01,885 --> 00:08:06,511
Privacy preferences choose automatic, local-preferred, or remote-
preferred processing.

80
00:08:06,511 --> 00:08:10,559
For this remote-only workflow, choose remote processing.

81
00:08:10,559 --> 00:08:19,233
Save customizations; they apply to new recordings and newly generated
analyses without changing existing results.

82
00:08:19,733 --> 00:08:21,072
Choose New recording.

83
00:08:21,072 --> 00:08:28,214
Select an existing project or create a new one, then give the meeting a
descriptive name.

84
00:08:28,214 --> 00:08:32,232
The preview shows the folder that will be created.

85
00:08:32,232 --> 00:08:36,695
Continue to the three-step workspace: Capture, Transcript, and AI
analysis.

86
00:08:36,695 --> 00:08:46,069
In Capture, choose the spoken language, the microphone for the room
track, and the output whose monitor should supply system audio.

87
00:08:46,069 --> 00:08:48,747
Pick the output you actually hear.

88
00:08:48,747 --> 00:08:58,567
A Bluetooth headset microphone may force the headset into a lower-
quality capture profile, so a wired or built-in microphone is often
clearer.

89
00:08:58,567 --> 00:09:03,923
Enable Live transcript while recording only when a running preview is
useful.

90
00:09:03,923 --> 00:09:08,833
The final saved recording is transcribed again regardless of that
toggle.

91
00:09:09,333 --> 00:09:10,662
Press Start recording.

92
00:09:10,662 --> 00:09:17,750
The workspace replaces its setup cards with a timer, waveform, recording
controls, and the live transcript.

93
00:09:17,750 --> 00:09:22,623
The level meter reflects the audio actually reaching S I T.

94
00:09:22,623 --> 00:09:29,711
If it remains silent for several seconds, the interface tells you to
check the selected microphone.

95
00:09:29,711 --> 00:09:37,242
The first live text normally appears after about ten seconds of captured
audio plus model processing time.

96
00:09:37,242 --> 00:09:42,114
Later updates add new audio while retaining a short context window.

97
00:09:42,114 --> 00:09:46,101
The newest provisional segment is highlighted and may change.

98
00:09:46,101 --> 00:09:53,189
If processing falls behind, the status shows the delay; slow processing
does not intentionally skip segments.

99
00:09:53,189 --> 00:09:56,733
Cancel discards the temporary audio and live preview.

100
00:09:56,733 --> 00:10:03,378
Stop and save moves the recording into the library and starts the
complete transcription workflow.

101
00:10:03,378 --> 00:10:08,694
If the recording reaches its time limit, it remains available to save.

102
00:10:08,694 --> 00:10:13,567
Do not close the backend while saving or processing is active.

103
00:10:14,067 --> 00:10:19,752
To process an existing file, choose Upload existing audio instead of
recording.

104
00:10:19,752 --> 00:10:30,649
S I T accepts common audio formats and video, then uses FFmpeg to
normalize the source into a library W A V file.

105
00:10:30,649 --> 00:10:35,387
Upload works even when the host has no PulseAudio recorder.

106
00:10:35,387 --> 00:10:39,177
After capture or upload, open the Transcript step.

107
00:10:39,177 --> 00:10:42,967
Confirm the language and expected number of speakers.

108
00:10:42,967 --> 00:10:49,600
Leave the count empty for automatic detection, or use one to skip
speaker recognition.

109
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:54,811
Generate transcript sends the audio to the configured remote
transcription endpoint.

110
00:10:54,811 --> 00:11:00,497
When speaker recognition is enabled, the diarizer labels the timed
segments afterward.

111
00:11:00,497 --> 00:11:06,182
The progress view reports transcription, diarization, merging,
completion, or a real error.

112
00:11:06,182 --> 00:11:13,762
In this demonstration, both operations use the working Spark tunnels;
the local model is not used.

113
00:11:13,762 --> 00:11:18,500
You can rerun speaker recognition later without repeating speech
recognition.

114
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,989
Open the finished meeting to review timed transcript cues.

115
00:11:22,989 --> 00:11:27,421
Selecting a timestamp seeks the audio player to that moment.

116
00:11:27,421 --> 00:11:31,410
Choose Edit meeting to correct individual words or labels.

117
00:11:31,410 --> 00:11:35,842
Every cue must retain text before it can be saved.

118
00:11:35,842 --> 00:11:41,603
Transcript edits mark any existing AI analysis as outdated because the
source changed.

119
00:11:41,603 --> 00:11:49,581
The Speakers tab groups detected labels and lets you replace them with
real names across the whole transcript.

120
00:11:49,581 --> 00:11:53,570
Save speaker names once, rather than editing every cue.

121
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:59,774
If separation was incorrect, enter an expected speaker count and choose
Recognize speakers again.

122
00:11:59,774 --> 00:12:04,206
This replaces speaker labels but keeps the existing speech-recognition
text.

123
00:12:04,206 --> 00:12:14,400
The Transcript tab can regenerate everything with a different language
or speaker count, but that operation replaces the current transcript and
derived analysis.

124
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:19,089
Continue to AI analysis after the transcript is ready.

125
00:12:19,089 --> 00:12:28,863
Use the saved defaults or expand Adjust this analysis to change detail,
output language, instructions, and requested sections for this meeting.

126
00:12:28,863 --> 00:12:37,706
The available structured sections include a timeline, decisions, action
items, open questions, risks, speaker contributions, and a follow-up
draft.

127
00:12:37,706 --> 00:12:43,291
Generate AI analysis runs in the background against the configured
OpenAI-compatible service.

128
00:12:43,291 --> 00:12:46,549
There is no built-in local analysis fallback.

129
00:12:46,549 --> 00:12:55,393
The finished brief links important claims back to source timestamps, so
selecting a source jumps to the relevant audio.

130
00:12:55,393 --> 00:13:05,167
If you edit the transcript afterward, S I T preserves the previous
analysis but marks it outdated until you regenerate it.

131
00:13:05,667 --> 00:13:09,146
The Library groups meetings below project folders.

132
00:13:09,146 --> 00:13:17,596
Search the current folder, create subfolders, and use each item menu to
move, rename, or delete content.

133
00:13:17,596 --> 00:13:26,047
A meeting can contain audio, plain transcript text, structured timed
segments, Markdown analysis, and structured analysis data.

134
00:13:26,047 --> 00:13:32,011
Opening a finished meeting restores its audio player, provenance,
transcript, and analysis.

135
00:13:32,011 --> 00:13:39,964
Open on disk requires the optional transcriber opener helper; every
other library feature works without it.

136
00:13:39,964 --> 00:13:43,941
You can always open the data directory manually.

137
00:13:43,941 --> 00:13:50,900
Keep backups because this is alpha software and the on-disk layout may
still change.

138
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:56,339
By default, the library lives at dot local share slash sit.

139
00:13:56,339 --> 00:14:07,564
Transcripts use dot txt files, timed data uses dot meeting dot json,
recordings live in an audio subfolder, and analyses use Markdown and
JSON sidecars.

140
00:14:07,564 --> 00:14:10,258
Temporary work stays under underscore scratch.

141
00:14:10,258 --> 00:14:20,585
When remote processing is enabled, audio is sent to the transcription
and diarization servers, and transcript text is sent to the analysis
server.

142
00:14:20,585 --> 00:14:23,728
Use only infrastructure appropriate for that data.

143
00:14:23,728 --> 00:14:29,565
Connection secrets stay server-side; the Hugging Face token is write-
only in the browser.

144
00:14:29,565 --> 00:14:35,851
The FastAPI service has no authentication and intentionally binds to
local host by default.

145
00:14:35,851 --> 00:14:38,096
Do not expose it publicly.

146
00:14:38,096 --> 00:14:47,076
For recording failures, verify that pactl info can see the desktop audio
server and that FFmpeg and pactl are installed.

147
00:14:47,076 --> 00:14:51,117
For connection failures, test SSH first, then each service.

148
00:14:51,117 --> 00:14:55,607
Connection refused usually means the tunnel or service is absent.

149
00:14:55,607 --> 00:14:58,750
A four-oh-four points to the API path.

150
00:14:58,750 --> 00:15:04,138
A model error means the configured model does not match the server.

151
00:15:04,138 --> 00:15:13,567
If a local port is occupied, close the process that owns the old tunnel
or assign a different unique local port.

152
00:15:14,067 --> 00:15:20,435
To update, close S I T and run update dot sh from the checkout.

153
00:15:20,435 --> 00:15:30,443
It performs a fast-forward pull, refreshes Python dependencies, rebuilds
the native application when Tauri sources changed, and updates an
installed desktop entry.

154
00:15:30,443 --> 00:15:34,536
Settings, the environment, and library data remain in place.

155
00:15:34,536 --> 00:15:42,270
The script refuses to update while the backend is running and refuses to
pull across uncommitted changes.

156
00:15:42,270 --> 00:15:54,551
Use dash dash no pull when you deliberately want to reinstall the
current working tree, and dash dash force build when the native binary
must be rebuilt.

157
00:15:54,551 --> 00:16:06,833
You now have a remote-only S I T installation with managed SSH tunnels,
recording and upload workflows, speaker-aware transcripts, structured AI
analysis, and a maintainable local library.
